atchRatio":0.4961},{"ratio":0.81,"watchRatio":0.4914},{"ratio":0.82,"watchRatio":0.4902},{"ratio":0.83,"watchRatio":0.4861},{"ratio":0.84,"watchRatio":0.48450000000000004},{"ratio":0.85,"watchRatio":0.48119999999999996},{"ratio":0.86,"watchRatio":0.47950000000000004},{"ratio":0.87,"watchRatio":0.4765},{"ratio":0.88,"watchRatio":0.4693},{"ratio":0.89,"watchRatio":0.4664},{"ratio":0.9,"watchRatio":0.4646},{"ratio":0.91,"watchRatio":0.4585},{"ratio":0.92,"watchRatio":0.4531},{"ratio":0.93,"watchRatio":0.44799999999999995},{"ratio":0.94,"watchRatio":0.4457},{"ratio":0.95,"watchRatio":0.4391},{"ratio":0.96,"watchRatio":0.4322},{"ratio":0.97,"watchRatio":0.4233},{"ratio":0.98,"watchRatio":0.41100000000000003},{"ratio":0.99,"watchRatio":0.3973},{"ratio":1,"watchRatio":0.3918}],"script":{"source_path":"all-146-tagged.json","words":187,"wpm":null,"lang":"en","chapters":[],"blocks":[],"text":"The morning after he bombed the Viceroy of India, he was back at his desk telling his British boss how horrifying the attack was. His name was Rash Behari Bose, a soft-spoken government clerk who ran a secret revolutionary cell across North India. In 1912, his men threw bomb at Viceroy. Bose caught the overnight train home and opened his office on time. The police never suspected the quiet clerk. Three years later, his plot for a nationwide army mutiny was betrayed by an informant. With the Raj hunting him, Bose shaved his beard and fled to Japan under a false identity. British agents demanded his deportation. Instead, a Tokyo's banker family hid the empire's most wanted man above their shop. He learned fluent Japanese, married the baker's daughter, and became a Japanese citizen. Then, he began organizing Indian [music] exiles and prisoners of war into an army. In 1943, he handed that over to another Bose, Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose. India knows it as INA. Rash Behari Bose died in Tokyo [music] in January 1945. The clerk who fooled an empire never saw his homeland free."}}